in 1980 Stephen King published one of  his most disturbing stories a novella  called the mist the story got even more  disturbing when filmmaker Frank Darabont  wrote a new ending for his 2007 movie  adaptation the film's final twist  devastated audiences and it still  traumatized as people today here's what  it all means so considering this movie's  over 10 years old let's do a quick recap  so we're all on the same page the mist  tells the story of a dad named David and  his son Billy who get caught inside a  supermarket when a mysterious and  dangerous mist engulfs a small town in  Maine over the course of the film  we learn a few things the mist is full  of horrors which include deadly  tentacles and giant wasps and  pterodactyls we also learned that one of  the stores patrons Amanda Dumfries  carries a gun but before we skip to the  end there's one important moment that we  need to touch on before all the monsters  show up but after the parking lot is  filled with fog everybody agrees to stay  inside except for one unnamed woman  played by Melissa McBride she's a mom  who left her kids at home to do some  quick grocery shopping but with the  world falling apart she can't sit around  twiddling her thumbs  she's got to get back to her kids  several people tried to stop her but she  won't hear it and when she asked for  help everybody goes quiet teary-eyed and  disgusted she curses the group and she  strides into the mist having faith she  can get home in one piece it's a  nerve-wracking and heartbreaking moment  and one will need to file away for  future reference this moment comes  around full circle and hammers some  themes down hard eventually after the  mist starts belching up monsters  tentacles and tons of baby spiders  neighborhood nutjob mrs. Carmody a  bloodthirsty bible-thumper who thinks  the end times are nigh  starts gathering followers who oppose  our hero David and his allies attempts  to stay smart and survive during a  crisis suffice it to say this whole mist  thing is pretty bad news guys eventually  the survivors inside the supermarket  begin to turn on each other  Lord of the Flies style a small group of  allies join David and Billy as they  escape from the store in the hopes that  whatever's lurking in the mist isn't as  bad as the horror of human beings that  they're worse  several of them are picked off by killer  bugs but the surviving members david  billy and three others named amanda dan  and irene pile into a car and drive into  the fog but not before david grabs  Amanda's gun as they drive down the road  they see toppled telephone poles wrecked  cars and a school bus savaged by spiders  they find the webbed up body of David's  dead wife watch an enormous Lovecraftian  creature lumber by and no matter how far  they drive the mist stretches on forever  eventually they run out of gas and as  they hear the monsters outside they make  a decision a bullet is better than a bug  David checks the gun sees there are only  four bullets for five people and does  the deed he kills everyone in the car  including his son David is the only one  left alive howling like a wounded animal  then he starts begging for the monsters  to finish him off  but his despair deepens when seconds  later a military convoy rumbles out of  the Mist adding insult to injury david  seized vehicles full of survivors  including Melissa McBride and her kids  the mist clears and David is left  screaming in pain if only he'd waited a  few more seconds his son would still be  alive  [Music]  and on that cheery note the film fades  to black if you couldn't tell by the end  of the film the mist doesn't think very  highly of mankind it shares a worldview  with movies like Night of the Living  Dead and John Carpenter's The Thing  according to the mist people are prone  to paranoia and distrust and will turn  on each other when things go south but  at the same time the movie is ultimately  a message about the power of hope and  what happens when Hope disappears  speaking with Yahoo Entertainment Frank  Darabont explained the mist works as a  companion piece with his first feature  the Shawshank Redemption  if Shawshank is the movie about the  value of hope then the mist becomes a  movie about the danger of hopelessness  so in the 94 classic Andy Dufresne hangs  on to hope and escapes from prison  because it comes down to a simple choice  really busy live but in the midst  instead of getting busy living David and  his companions do the other one while  Andy keeps the faith and finds freedom  David gives up and loses everything when  salvation was so close and this is where  Melissa McBride's character comes back  in this mom marched into the fog to  rescue her kids even though everyone  else was scared and tried to stop her  she never let go of hope and as a result  she saved her children David on the  other hand fell into despair and lost  his son's life so what's the big moral  of the Mist even if life feels like a  never-ending nightmare be like that mom  because you never know when the mist  will fade away both the novella and  movie versions of the mist are excellent  but they radically different comes to  the ending while the film goes in a  decidedly dark direction King's novella  is much more ambiguous in fact the  author actually leaves his readers with  some hope that things will work out for  David and his crew team David escapes  the supermarket but instead of  committing mass suicide David turns on  the radio and thinks he hears a voice  say the word Harford encouraged he  believes Hartford Connecticut might be a  safe zone and he heads in that direction  the novella ends with David narrating  these final lines I'm going to bed now  but first I'm going to kiss my son and  whisper two words in his ear two words  that sound a bit  like one of them is Hartford the other  is hope and that's where the book ends  hinting that maybe team David is going  to be okay  obviously Darabont decided to traumatize  moviegoers but Stephen King was totally  okay with the movie's new ending when  Darabont asked for king's opinion on the  nihilistic finale the author gave him  two thumbs up King even famously  declared that quote anybody who reveals  the last five minutes of this film  should be hung from their neck until  dead okay  we really hope he doesn't watch this  video check out one of our newest videos  right here plus even more looper videos  about your favorite movies are coming  soon subscribe to our YouTube channel  and hit the bell so you don't miss a  single one  [Music]
 

